{"id": "tea:sarvarthasiddhi:1.1", "source": "src:sarvarthasiddhi", "location": {"ref": "1.1", "chapter": "1", "verse": "1", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Right view and right knowledge arise together, as the light and heat of a lamp arise together; right conduct may follow after.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "causal", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["karma-liberation"], "concepts": ["cpt:three-jewels"], "teachers": ["tch:pujyapada"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "Recalled; the simile and its exact location to be checked.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "low", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:sarvarthasiddhi:1.8", "source": "src:sarvarthasiddhi", "location": {"ref": "1.8", "chapter": "1", "verse": "8", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "In explaining the eight doors of inquiry, the commentary names the fourteen stages of quality (guṇasthāna) — from wrong view to the omniscient without activity — and examines each soul-type through them.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["karma-liberation", "practice"], "terms": ["trm:gunasthana"], "concepts": ["cpt:fourteen-gunasthanas"], "paths": ["pth:jain-fourteen-gunasthanas"], "teachers": ["tch:pujyapada"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:sarvarthasiddhi:10.9", "source": "src:sarvarthasiddhi", "location": {"ref": "10.9"}, "paraphrase": "On the 'sign' (liṅga) of the liberated: with respect to physical sex (dravya-veda) liberation is only from the male; with respect to psychological sex (bhāva-veda) it may be from any of the three; with respect to garb, liberation is only in the state without possessions (nirgrantha-liṅga), though the past garb may be considered either way.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["knowledge"], "stage": "realized"}, "types": ["dispute", "karma-liberation"], "teachers": ["tch:pujyapada"], "concepts": ["cpt:strimukti", "cpt:dravya-bhava-veda"], "disputes": ["dsp:women-caste-liberation", "dsp:monastic-nudity"], "notes": "Moderate confidence on the formulation.", "reported_by_opponent": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U34-jain-canon"], "disagreements": []}, "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U34-jain-canon"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:sarvarthasiddhi:7.22", "source": "src:sarvarthasiddhi", "location": {"ref": "7.22", "chapter": "7", "verse": "22", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "Sallekhanā is not self-killing: it is undertaken free of attachment when death has become inevitable, as a merchant who does not wish his house destroyed still saves his goods when it is burning; the one who kills himself does so from passion.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["action"], "stage": "advanced"}, "types": ["death-dying", "ethics"], "concepts": ["cpt:sallekhana"], "practices": ["prc:sallekhana"], "teachers": ["tch:pujyapada"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "restricted": true, "notes": "Restricted: the text's own conditions and reasoning only. Merchant simile recalled.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:sarvarthasiddhi:8.1", "source": "src:sarvarthasiddhi", "location": {"ref": "8.1"}, "paraphrase": "Wrong faith taught by others is of four kinds — the doctrines of action, of non-action, of ignorance and of discipline — or, again, of five kinds: one-sided (ekānta), perverse (viparīta), doubting (saṃśaya), discipline-alone (vainayika) and ignorant (ajñāna).", "tags": {"level": "unmarked", "standpoint": "analytic", "naya": null, "path": ["general"], "stage": "unmarked"}, "types": ["dispute", "karma-liberation"], "terms": ["trm:mithyatva"], "concepts": ["cpt:four-vada-classes"], "obstacles": ["obs:mithyatva"], "teachers": ["tch:pujyapada"], "reported_by_opponent": true, "notes": "Reported by an opponent: Pūjyapāda (Digambara). Commentary on Tattvārtha Sūtra 8.1; the five-fold list is well known, its exact wording in SS is recalled.", "coverage": ["B1", "B2"], "ai_translated": false, "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U33-sramana"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U33-sramana"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:sarvarthasiddhi:9.11", "source": "src:sarvarthasiddhi", "location": {"ref": "9.11", "chapter": "9", "verse": "11", "section": null}, "paraphrase": "The eleven hardships are said of the Jina only figuratively: feeling karma, without the support of delusion, cannot produce hunger and the rest in the omniscient.", "tags": {"level": "unmarked", "standpoint": "polemical", "naya": null, "path": ["action"], "stage": "realized"}, "types": ["powers-experiences", "dispute"], "concepts": ["cpt:kevalin-nature"], "teachers": ["tch:pujyapada"], "disputes": ["dsp:kevalin-eats"], "commentary_on": "tea:tattvartha-sutra:9.11", "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "moderate", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
{"id": "tea:sarvarthasiddhi:mangala", "source": "src:sarvarthasiddhi", "location": {"ref": "mangala", "section": null}, "original": {"text": "mokṣamārgasya netāraṃ bhettāraṃ karmabhūbhṛtām / jñātāraṃ viśvatattvānāṃ vande tadguṇalabdhaye", "script": "IAST", "edition": "recalled by the unit author (wording believed standard; to be checked against an edition)", "licence": "public-domain text"}, "paraphrase": "I bow to the leader on the path of liberation, the breaker of the mountains of karma, the knower of all realities, in order to attain those very qualities.", "tags": {"level": "conventional", "standpoint": "devotional", "naya": null, "path": ["devotion", "knowledge"], "stage": "all"}, "types": ["teacher-transmission", "karma-liberation"], "terms": ["trm:moksa-marga", "trm:arhat"], "concepts": ["cpt:pancaparamesthin"], "teachers": ["tch:pujyapada"], "fidelity": {"status": "unchecked"}, "extraction": {"method": "skeleton", "extractors": ["U35-jain-philosophy"], "disagreements": []}, "correction_log": [], "notes": "The Jina is praised as the model whose qualities the devotee seeks to realise in himself, not as a giver of grace.", "coverage": ["B2"], "verification": {"level": "skeleton", "confidence": "high", "unverified": false, "checks": []}, "provenance": {"units": ["skeleton:U35-jain-philosophy"], "phases": ["skeleton"]}}
